WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.251 --> 00:00:02.689
- 'Cause I feel like everybody
always treats actors like,

00:00:02.689 --> 00:00:04.911
as they probably should,
emotional, like, landmines,

00:00:04.911 --> 00:00:07.242
everybody is like tiptoeing
around because you never know

00:00:07.242 --> 00:00:08.075
what's going to set them off,

00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:09.990
and it's just a job, like any other.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.031
You look how you look, and be comfortable.

00:00:12.031 --> 00:00:14.096
Like what are you going to
do, be hungry every single day

00:00:14.096 --> 00:00:15.186
to make other people happy?

00:00:15.186 --> 00:00:16.161
That's just dumb.

00:00:16.161 --> 00:00:19.312
My childhood in my mind seems so lovely,

00:00:19.312 --> 00:00:22.129
and just picturesque, and
then I talk back about it,

00:00:22.129 --> 00:00:23.583
I'm like, "Oh my God."

00:00:23.583 --> 00:00:26.140
- She's the highest paid
actress in the world

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.044
and her films have grossed
over five billion dollars.

00:00:30.044 --> 00:00:32.357
She had her breakthrough in 2010

00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:34.277
playing a poverty-stricken teenager

00:00:34.277 --> 00:00:37.079
in the independent film "Winter's Bone."

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:40.011
In 2013, she was listed in Time Magazine's

00:00:40.011 --> 00:00:42.993
100 Most Influential People in the World.

00:00:42.993 --> 00:00:44.877
She's Jennifer Lawrence,
and here's my take

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:46.886
on her top 10 rules for success.

00:00:46.886 --> 00:00:48.403
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:48.403 --> 00:00:50.049
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.652
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:51.652 --> 00:00:53.297
Also, as Jennifer's talking,
if she says something

00:00:53.297 --> 00:00:54.978
that really resonates with you,

00:00:54.978 --> 00:00:56.452
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:56.452 --> 00:00:58.008
and put quotes around it so other people

00:00:58.008 --> 00:01:00.093
can be inspired as well.

00:01:00.093 --> 00:01:00.926
Enjoy.

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:03.756
(swoosh)

00:01:03.756 --> 00:01:06.506
(dramatic music)

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.538
- Hi, I'm Jennifer Lawrence.

00:01:15.538 --> 00:01:16.917
Have you been hiding under a rock?

00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:18.339
How would you not know that.

00:01:18.339 --> 00:01:22.506
I've always had this really
gross, dangerous mentality of,

00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:27.612
no consideration of failure.

00:01:27.612 --> 00:01:29.813
Just never even considering
the thought of failing,

00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:33.980
like, if I want something
I just go until I get it.

00:01:36.038 --> 00:01:38.871
And that doesn't always work, but.

00:01:40.104 --> 00:01:42.038
- The interesting thing
for me to talk to you,

00:01:42.038 --> 00:01:44.096
and what makes you so fascinating

00:01:44.096 --> 00:01:47.769
is just the talent that
you see on the screen,

00:01:47.769 --> 00:01:48.967
but at the same time,

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:53.582
there is a certain innocence
and yet a certain power,

00:01:53.582 --> 00:01:57.749
and it seems to me it's both
artist and also business.

00:02:01.543 --> 00:02:02.507
- Well thank you.

00:02:02.507 --> 00:02:03.568
- [Charlie] Do you believe that?

00:02:03.568 --> 00:02:04.401
- Yes.
- Am I right?

00:02:04.401 --> 00:02:07.602
- I've worked very hard
to make that (laughs)

00:02:07.602 --> 00:02:10.343
to make that true, so thank you.

00:02:10.343 --> 00:02:11.295
- [Charlie] That it's both that.

00:02:11.295 --> 00:02:13.791
- Thank you, because without gaining

00:02:13.791 --> 00:02:15.403
some sort of control over the business

00:02:15.403 --> 00:02:19.103
I lose some control over the creative,

00:02:19.103 --> 00:02:20.691
which is most important.

00:02:20.691 --> 00:02:21.764
- [Charlie] The more you
control the business,

00:02:21.764 --> 00:02:23.705
the more you can give
flower to the creative.

00:02:23.705 --> 00:02:25.012
- Exactly.

00:02:25.012 --> 00:02:26.048
- [Charlie] Or give wings to the creative.

00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:28.680
- Yeah, so I used to stay out of it.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.888
"I don't care.

00:02:29.888 --> 00:02:31.510
"I'm an artist.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.034
"I don't need it."

00:02:33.034 --> 00:02:36.627
But this is my business
now, and it is important.

00:02:36.627 --> 00:02:39.636
My agent always says I'm his only client

00:02:39.636 --> 00:02:41.856
that ever calls him back as soon as he,

00:02:41.856 --> 00:02:43.694
because it's my business,
and I respect my business.

00:02:43.694 --> 00:02:45.122
- [Charlie] Your agent
says you're his only client

00:02:45.122 --> 00:02:45.955
that calls him back to say--

00:02:45.955 --> 00:02:47.179
- Immediately.

00:02:47.179 --> 00:02:48.687
- Immediately.
- If I have a missed call,

00:02:48.687 --> 00:02:50.548
I'm probably the only,

00:02:50.548 --> 00:02:52.698
I return emails, I return
phone calls because--

00:02:52.698 --> 00:02:55.251
- [Charlie] Because you want
to know what options there are.

00:02:55.251 --> 00:02:57.581
- Yes, I've worked really
hard to build this,

00:02:57.581 --> 00:02:59.365
and I want to continue building it,

00:02:59.365 --> 00:03:01.923
and it's my business,
my personal business,

00:03:01.923 --> 00:03:04.297
so I don't understand how people do slack.

00:03:04.297 --> 00:03:07.741
- I'm really annoying
with how I choose things,

00:03:07.741 --> 00:03:10.948
'cause it's really like a feeling,

00:03:10.948 --> 00:03:11.980
which just sounds stupid,

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.147
but sometimes I've read
scripts before that have...

00:03:16.998 --> 00:03:19.611
I just recently have read a
script that I was sobbing.

00:03:19.611 --> 00:03:22.348
I've never been so moved by a story,

00:03:22.348 --> 00:03:24.926
and I thought it was the most
beautiful, amazing story.

00:03:24.926 --> 00:03:26.969
And I just couldn't wait
for this movie to get made

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:28.495
so that the world could see it,

00:03:28.495 --> 00:03:31.965
but I just wasn't her,
I wasn't the character,

00:03:31.965 --> 00:03:33.416
and I couldn't.

00:03:33.416 --> 00:03:34.762
And I also had an experience once

00:03:34.762 --> 00:03:35.946
where I read the script and I loved it,

00:03:35.946 --> 00:03:38.587
but I couldn't stop
picturing Amanda Seyfried,

00:03:38.587 --> 00:03:40.534
and so I emailed the
people and I was like,

00:03:40.534 --> 00:03:43.019
"You got to hire Amanda
Seyfried for this."

00:03:43.019 --> 00:03:44.067
I couldn't stop picturing it.

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:46.273
So a lot of it is the things that he said,

00:03:46.273 --> 00:03:47.646
you want to be part of a good film

00:03:47.646 --> 00:03:49.504
and you want to make a good movie,

00:03:49.504 --> 00:03:54.059
but there's also a huge
emotional component for me

00:03:54.059 --> 00:03:56.038
that I don't know how to describe it.

00:03:56.038 --> 00:03:58.208
It's just kind of
sometimes something clicks

00:03:58.208 --> 00:04:00.633
and I feel like that person does

00:04:00.633 --> 00:04:03.537
in a weird way exist in me,

00:04:03.537 --> 00:04:05.424
and it sounds like a stupid thing to say,

00:04:05.424 --> 00:04:06.544
and sometimes it just doesn't,

00:04:06.544 --> 00:04:08.379
and sometimes I can read a character

00:04:08.379 --> 00:04:09.212
and absolutely love her,

00:04:09.212 --> 00:04:13.788
but I know that I could never,
I'm not her or something,

00:04:13.788 --> 00:04:14.649
I don't know.

00:04:14.649 --> 00:04:15.602
- [Interviewer] So how was the experience

00:04:15.602 --> 00:04:16.997
of being directed by David finally?

00:04:16.997 --> 00:04:19.590
- Oh, a dream come true,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.585
'cause when you're such
a huge fan of somebody

00:04:21.585 --> 00:04:22.543
you build it up so much

00:04:22.543 --> 00:04:24.716
and you're always prepared to be let down,

00:04:24.716 --> 00:04:28.996
but he was just the nicest,
warmest, sweetest person

00:04:28.996 --> 00:04:29.974
in the entire world,

00:04:29.974 --> 00:04:32.807
just has such a warm, good energy,

00:04:34.717 --> 00:04:37.437
and then he just directs in this,

00:04:37.437 --> 00:04:38.736
I've never worked that way,

00:04:38.736 --> 00:04:40.880
and that's such an incredible thing to do

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.609
is to kind of realize that
there's no one or right way

00:04:44.609 --> 00:04:47.295
to make a movie or direct an actor.

00:04:47.295 --> 00:04:48.757
- [Interviewer] So did he do something

00:04:48.757 --> 00:04:50.285
that you particularly liked
that you haven't done?

00:04:50.285 --> 00:04:52.859
- Yeah, he would interrupt
like any monologue,

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:55.021
no matter what it was, no
matter how serious, he would go,

00:04:55.021 --> 00:04:56.945
"Oh, it's such bullshit.

00:04:56.945 --> 00:05:00.523
"It's so bad, it's so bad." (laughs)

00:05:00.523 --> 00:05:03.080
I'd be like, "Alright, let's do it again."

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.876
It's just like so funny
to be able to like,

00:05:05.876 --> 00:05:08.229
because you can be honest
like on this whole new level.

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:10.674
That's why Bradley and I don't
want to stop working together,

00:05:10.674 --> 00:05:12.908
because we can just be
like, "Don't say that.

00:05:12.908 --> 00:05:13.741
"Don't wear that.

00:05:13.741 --> 00:05:14.748
"What are you doing?"

00:05:14.748 --> 00:05:17.586
Just boss each other around.

00:05:17.586 --> 00:05:19.990
Yeah, I like him not wasting time

00:05:19.990 --> 00:05:21.261
and just like cutting the bullshit,

00:05:21.261 --> 00:05:22.750
and just getting real,

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.576
'cause I feel like everybody
always treats actors like,

00:05:25.576 --> 00:05:27.590
as they probably should,
emotional like landmines,

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.383
everybody is like tip toeing around

00:05:29.383 --> 00:05:31.178
'cause you never know what's
going to set them off.

00:05:31.178 --> 00:05:32.590
It's just a job like any other.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:33.511
Sometimes you're going to be good,

00:05:33.511 --> 00:05:35.036
and sometimes you're going to be bad.

00:05:35.036 --> 00:05:37.456
I like it when somebody
is like, "That was bad.

00:05:37.456 --> 00:05:38.664
"Do it again."

00:05:38.664 --> 00:05:40.785
- [Interviewer] So when he's saying that

00:05:40.785 --> 00:05:43.323
he's saying it mostly about
you guys, not about his script?

00:05:43.323 --> 00:05:44.554
- No, it's never about a script.

00:05:44.554 --> 00:05:46.475
It's always about our
performance. (laughs)

00:05:46.475 --> 00:05:47.523
- [Interviewer] You just missed the mark.

00:05:47.523 --> 00:05:48.636
- Yeah no, it'll just be like,

00:05:48.636 --> 00:05:50.721
"You were really bad."

00:05:50.721 --> 00:05:52.569
The scariest part of my job

00:05:52.569 --> 00:05:55.402
is the gamble of reading a script.

00:05:57.836 --> 00:05:59.427
Which one do you do?

00:05:59.427 --> 00:06:00.260
Who makes it?

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:01.093
You never know.

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:03.211
You can read an amazing script

00:06:03.211 --> 00:06:06.713
and hire a director who seems fantastic,

00:06:06.713 --> 00:06:08.132
and have an amazing co star,

00:06:08.132 --> 00:06:11.126
and you can be on set and
be completely out of control

00:06:11.126 --> 00:06:12.633
and watch it either fall apart,

00:06:12.633 --> 00:06:13.900
or you can love it

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.690
and then audiences don't respond,

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:19.773
and then there is planning of a year.

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:22.555
You know, if you're going
to have this movie come out,

00:06:22.555 --> 00:06:23.994
what follows that, and then what?

00:06:23.994 --> 00:06:26.927
And that's the most
stressful part of my job,

00:06:26.927 --> 00:06:27.760
'cause it is a gamble,

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:31.459
but it's important to me that
I do make those decisions.

00:06:31.459 --> 00:06:35.404
I don't want an agent's
career or a lawyer's career.

00:06:35.404 --> 00:06:36.302
I need advice.

00:06:36.302 --> 00:06:38.770
I have an amazing agent and
I really respect his opinion,

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:40.687
but I would never do...

00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:47.121
My entire career is dictated by a feeling,

00:06:47.121 --> 00:06:49.397
and I've gone through great lengths

00:06:49.397 --> 00:06:51.161
to try and make sure
that it stays that way.

00:06:51.161 --> 00:06:53.744
I've shed big parts of my team,

00:06:55.442 --> 00:06:59.559
'cause I don't want more
people to say no to.

00:06:59.559 --> 00:07:00.664
- [Charlie] You trust your gut?

00:07:00.664 --> 00:07:03.852
- I do, and sometimes I'm wrong.

00:07:03.852 --> 00:07:07.081
- And when you're wrong,
what do you think happens?

00:07:07.081 --> 00:07:08.662
- Everything is fine, it happens.

00:07:08.662 --> 00:07:10.097
In business there's ups and downs.

00:07:10.097 --> 00:07:12.281
I would much rather blame
myself though than go,

00:07:12.281 --> 00:07:13.501
"I shouldn't have
listened to that person."

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:14.925
At least I go, "Well, you learned."

00:07:14.925 --> 00:07:15.893
- You know why you made the choice

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:16.726
even if it turned out
to be the wrong choice.

00:07:16.726 --> 00:07:17.603
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:18.972
- But when I just started

00:07:18.972 --> 00:07:21.764
and I had no idea what I was doing,

00:07:21.764 --> 00:07:24.271
I didn't what else to do,
I didn't know how to act,

00:07:24.271 --> 00:07:25.981
I just knew how to talk,

00:07:25.981 --> 00:07:29.284
and I think once I started
understanding I was like,

00:07:29.284 --> 00:07:31.404
"Well, I don't really
want to learn how to act.

00:07:31.404 --> 00:07:33.061
"I just want to keep
learning how to talk."

00:07:33.061 --> 00:07:37.502
And I've always just been
such a people studier

00:07:37.502 --> 00:07:40.203
that I think that for me

00:07:40.203 --> 00:07:42.085
the best schooling I
could have ever gotten

00:07:42.085 --> 00:07:44.794
was watching and listening to the world.

00:07:44.794 --> 00:07:46.456
I mean just watching people talk,

00:07:46.456 --> 00:07:48.061
watching people do different things.

00:07:48.061 --> 00:07:51.704
So many of the things that I do on screen

00:07:51.704 --> 00:07:53.341
are things that I've seen other people do

00:07:53.341 --> 00:07:54.803
that I thought were interesting.

00:07:54.803 --> 00:07:56.847
you know, like funny things
with people's mouths,

00:07:56.847 --> 00:07:59.394
or when they're upset the
things they do with their hands.

00:07:59.394 --> 00:08:02.644
Like this one time my friend was crying

00:08:03.666 --> 00:08:04.982
and she was so upset.

00:08:04.982 --> 00:08:05.815
She put her hands up

00:08:05.815 --> 00:08:07.330
and she didn't know what to do with them

00:08:07.330 --> 00:08:08.374
and they were just shaking.

00:08:08.374 --> 00:08:09.347
And I was comforting her,

00:08:09.347 --> 00:08:10.180
but at the same time I was like,

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.824
"That's kind of cool." (laughs)

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:13.658
- Do it again, do it again, yeah.

00:08:13.658 --> 00:08:14.491
- Yes, yeah, I know.

00:08:14.491 --> 00:08:16.492
It was like, "What did
you do right there?"

00:08:16.492 --> 00:08:18.978
So I had an emotional
scene in The Poker House

00:08:18.978 --> 00:08:21.869
and almost every time I cried
I did that with my hands,

00:08:21.869 --> 00:08:24.366
and I don't know, I just
pick up from people.

00:08:24.366 --> 00:08:28.857
- Having experienced the body
image criticism in Hollywood,

00:08:28.857 --> 00:08:32.310
what is your advice to young girls

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:35.916
dealing with the same
treatment from their peers,

00:08:35.916 --> 00:08:40.083
and response to those who judge
others based on appearances?

00:08:41.197 --> 00:08:42.381
- Well screw those people.

00:08:42.381 --> 00:08:44.964
(audience laughs)

00:08:44.964 --> 00:08:45.797
- No, and it--
(applause)

00:08:45.797 --> 00:08:48.880
Oh, thanks. (laughs)

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:52.216
Yeah, I mean it's something
that everybody experiences.

00:08:52.216 --> 00:08:56.571
I experienced it in school
before I was famous.

00:08:56.571 --> 00:08:58.579
The world has a certain idea of,

00:08:58.579 --> 00:09:00.779
you know, we see this
airbrushed perfect model,

00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:02.333
and then if you don't
look like that, you're..

00:09:02.333 --> 00:09:05.426
And it's just too easy for people.

00:09:05.426 --> 00:09:07.365
I've got a million ideas
coming at the same time,

00:09:07.365 --> 00:09:10.198
but you just have to look past it.

00:09:11.394 --> 00:09:13.467
You look how you look, and be comfortable.

00:09:13.467 --> 00:09:15.528
Like what are you going to
do, be hungry every single day

00:09:15.528 --> 00:09:16.563
to make other people happy?

00:09:16.563 --> 00:09:18.023
That's just dumb.

00:09:18.023 --> 00:09:20.981
And the shows like the Fashion
Police and things like that

00:09:20.981 --> 00:09:25.447
are just showing these
generations of young people

00:09:25.447 --> 00:09:28.107
to judge people based on things,

00:09:28.107 --> 00:09:30.013
they put values on all
the things that are wrong,

00:09:30.013 --> 00:09:31.801
and that it's okay to just point at people

00:09:31.801 --> 00:09:33.193
and call them ugly and call them fat,

00:09:33.193 --> 00:09:35.943
and they call it fun, and
welcome to the real world,

00:09:35.943 --> 00:09:38.531
and it's like that
shouldn't be the real world.

00:09:38.531 --> 00:09:40.801
It's going to keep being the real
world if we keep it that way.

00:09:40.801 --> 00:09:44.289
It's not until we stop
treating each other like that,

00:09:44.289 --> 00:09:45.920
and just stop calling each other fat,

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.023
and stop with these unrealistic
expectations for women.

00:09:50.023 --> 00:09:54.762
It's disappointing that
the media keeps it alive

00:09:54.762 --> 00:09:56.538
and fuels that fire.

00:09:56.538 --> 00:09:59.243
It's something that really bothers me,

00:09:59.243 --> 00:10:01.076
because I love to eat.

00:10:02.122 --> 00:10:04.116
- What does it take to be a great actress?

00:10:04.116 --> 00:10:06.021
'Cause that performance in your movie

00:10:06.021 --> 00:10:09.752
is a really great performance
I think by any yardstick.

00:10:09.752 --> 00:10:11.007
What does she have, Jennifer,

00:10:11.007 --> 00:10:15.174
that takes it to that
kind of level? (laughs)

00:10:16.635 --> 00:10:20.802
- I think there's a soulfulness
that's immediately there.

00:10:22.353 --> 00:10:25.936
It's right there in her eyes
and in her face and in her,

00:10:25.936 --> 00:10:26.892
the way she talks.

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:28.149
I don't know how to express it

00:10:28.149 --> 00:10:30.008
except just to point to her performance.

00:10:30.008 --> 00:10:32.536
I mean, she has a soulfulness
that comes from her,

00:10:32.536 --> 00:10:33.710
and there's...

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:37.196
I don't want to embarrass her
or sound, I just think people...

00:10:37.196 --> 00:10:38.819
We were concerned she was
too young for the role,

00:10:38.819 --> 00:10:40.843
and I said she almost has
a timeless quality to her,

00:10:40.843 --> 00:10:41.880
which I do feel.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.632
I feel like sometimes
she seems like she's 18,

00:10:43.632 --> 00:10:45.525
sometimes she seems like she's 40.

00:10:45.525 --> 00:10:48.032
And not only that, a realness.

00:10:48.032 --> 00:10:49.856
There's a lack of preciousness.

00:10:49.856 --> 00:10:52.135
There's not a frame
around what she's doing.

00:10:52.135 --> 00:10:53.937
It's raw and it's real,

00:10:53.937 --> 00:10:56.325
and it comes from part of her soul,

00:10:56.325 --> 00:11:00.233
and she just channels it right out there.

00:11:00.233 --> 00:11:02.085
She's not afraid to jump in and do it

00:11:02.085 --> 00:11:03.684
any number of different ways,

00:11:03.684 --> 00:11:05.307
and she has very good instincts.

00:11:05.307 --> 00:11:08.382
She'll tell you when she
feels something is false

00:11:08.382 --> 00:11:10.401
and when she'd like to
find another way to do it.

00:11:10.401 --> 00:11:13.450
But it's a great gift to a
director to have an actor

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:16.662
who has so much emotion
readily available in their face

00:11:16.662 --> 00:11:18.663
that's authentic emotion.

00:11:18.663 --> 00:11:22.516
It's from her, and it's a part of her.

00:11:22.516 --> 00:11:23.509
I just don't know how...

00:11:23.509 --> 00:11:26.038
It's a blessing to have
someone channel that.

00:11:26.038 --> 00:11:28.632
- [Diane] And she's already
created a kind of legacy

00:11:28.632 --> 00:11:30.954
to be bold in your life.

00:11:30.954 --> 00:11:33.951
There was a girl she met
on the first Hunger Games,

00:11:33.951 --> 00:11:37.104
a burn survivor who had
scars all over her body.

00:11:37.104 --> 00:11:39.368
- She's said, "I love my body,

00:11:39.368 --> 00:11:41.981
"because I'm the girl on fire."

00:11:41.981 --> 00:11:44.510
It made her feel strong and brave,

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:48.677
and I just started crying,
'cause she gave me such a gift.

00:11:50.552 --> 00:11:54.728
And I'm just acting,
but it made me feel like

00:11:54.728 --> 00:11:58.392
if a character or a story
could make somebody change

00:11:58.392 --> 00:12:02.519
a perception on how they
feel about themselves,

00:12:02.519 --> 00:12:03.861
then that is beautiful.

00:12:03.861 --> 00:12:07.758
- [Diane] For a generation
of tiny fans, one message,

00:12:07.758 --> 00:12:09.425
believe in yourself.

00:12:11.449 --> 00:12:14.156
- I grew up on a horse farm,
so I rode a lot of horses,

00:12:14.156 --> 00:12:16.175
which I hear you like horses.

00:12:16.175 --> 00:12:17.008
- Yeah, I do, I like horses.

00:12:17.008 --> 00:12:19.074
- I hear you hate horses.

00:12:19.074 --> 00:12:20.667
- What, no, are you drunk?

00:12:20.667 --> 00:12:22.017
(audience laughs)

00:12:22.017 --> 00:12:23.571
- No, it just, it would've
been a very weird thing

00:12:23.571 --> 00:12:24.404
to hate horses.

00:12:24.404 --> 00:12:25.711
- No, I don't hate--
- They're so likeable.

00:12:25.711 --> 00:12:28.265
- I like 'em, but you ride them, right?

00:12:28.265 --> 00:12:29.098
- Yeah, I do.

00:12:29.098 --> 00:12:30.734
- You ever fallen off or been thrown?

00:12:30.734 --> 00:12:32.397
- Yeah, 'cause my parents were cheap,

00:12:32.397 --> 00:12:33.854
so they never bought trained horses.

00:12:33.854 --> 00:12:34.687
(audience laughs)

00:12:34.687 --> 00:12:36.632
So it was just kind of...

00:12:36.632 --> 00:12:38.115
I have a deformed tailbone, actually,

00:12:38.115 --> 00:12:39.360
from being thrown off of a horse.

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:40.318
- You know, I'd heard that.

00:12:40.318 --> 00:12:43.151
(audience laughs)

00:12:44.001 --> 00:12:47.167
Now wait a minute, you're
telling me that your parents

00:12:47.167 --> 00:12:51.593
bought wild, unbroken stallions
because they were cheaper

00:12:51.593 --> 00:12:53.083
and let the kids loose on 'em?

00:12:53.083 --> 00:12:53.916
(audience laughs)

00:12:53.916 --> 00:12:55.044
- Yeah, they were busy.

00:12:55.044 --> 00:12:55.877
They had more things to worry about.

00:12:55.877 --> 00:12:58.078
- They were busy, of course!

00:12:58.078 --> 00:12:59.968
So then you had to break your own horse.

00:12:59.968 --> 00:13:01.449
- Yeah, or my own tailbone.

00:13:01.449 --> 00:13:04.785
- And were you tossed or kicked?

00:13:04.785 --> 00:13:05.986
- Everything.

00:13:05.986 --> 00:13:09.371
At one point the horse
was running into a barn,

00:13:09.371 --> 00:13:10.481
like an abandoned barn,

00:13:10.481 --> 00:13:13.996
so I had to just jump
ship and drop and roll.

00:13:13.996 --> 00:13:16.218
That was the deformed elbow.

00:13:16.218 --> 00:13:17.051
(audience laughs)

00:13:17.051 --> 00:13:21.359
- In Kentucky, did they
ride Western or English?

00:13:21.359 --> 00:13:22.741
- I sound like such a redneck.

00:13:22.741 --> 00:13:25.756
I didn't really ride with a saddle.

00:13:25.756 --> 00:13:27.501
(audience laughs)

00:13:27.501 --> 00:13:29.183
We had English and Western saddles,

00:13:29.183 --> 00:13:30.799
but my mom said that
that was for the people

00:13:30.799 --> 00:13:32.157
who like board their horses there,

00:13:32.157 --> 00:13:33.381
so I wasn't allowed to use any of that,

00:13:33.381 --> 00:13:35.285
so I just kind of hopped
on and went for it.

00:13:35.285 --> 00:13:36.118
- Good idea.

00:13:36.118 --> 00:13:38.037
(audience laughs)

00:13:38.037 --> 00:13:40.119
Did anybody from the county
ever come out to the house?

00:13:40.119 --> 00:13:41.065
(Jennifer laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:13:41.065 --> 00:13:43.484
Talk to Mom and Dad about--

00:13:43.484 --> 00:13:47.364
- I'm going to get my parents arrested.

00:13:47.364 --> 00:13:50.530
My childhood in my mind seems so lovely

00:13:50.530 --> 00:13:51.799
and just picturesque,

00:13:51.799 --> 00:13:54.186
and then I talk back about
it I'm like, "Oh my God."

00:13:54.186 --> 00:13:55.712
- You did stay away from them saddles.

00:13:55.712 --> 00:13:58.318
Now get out there and break them broncs!

00:13:58.318 --> 00:13:59.296
- That's for the ranch folk.

00:13:59.296 --> 00:14:01.851
(Dave laughs)

00:14:01.851 --> 00:14:03.310
- Yeah, (mumbles) is for rich people.

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:05.227
- Oh my goodness, yeah.

00:14:07.506 --> 00:14:08.889
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:08.889 --> 00:14:13.002
I made this video because
MrPlaythroughs101 asked me to.

00:14:13.002 --> 00:14:14.139
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:14.139 --> 00:14:15.794
that you want me to profile next,

00:14:15.794 --> 00:14:17.132
leave it down in the comments below

00:14:17.132 --> 00:14:18.217
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:18.217 --> 00:14:20.242
I'd also love to know what
did you take from this video?

00:14:20.242 --> 00:14:23.334
What did Jennifer say that
had the biggest impact on you

00:14:23.334 --> 00:14:24.820
that you're going to
make an immediate change

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:26.618
to your business or your life?

00:14:26.618 --> 00:14:27.779
Leave it in the comments.

00:14:27.779 --> 00:14:29.482
I'm going to join in the conversation.

00:14:29.482 --> 00:14:31.804
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Kemo Tedajo.

00:14:31.804 --> 00:14:32.637
Thank you so much

00:14:32.637 --> 00:14:35.085
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:14:35.085 --> 00:14:38.025
and taking that awesome
picture with the Eiffel Tower.

00:14:38.025 --> 00:14:39.659
I really, really, really appreciate it.

00:14:39.659 --> 00:14:41.055
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:14:41.055 --> 00:14:42.458
I believe in you.

00:14:42.458 --> 00:14:44.809
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:14:44.809 --> 00:14:47.977
and whatever your one word is, much love.

00:14:47.977 --> 00:14:49.359
I'll see you soon.

00:14:49.359 --> 00:14:51.442
(swoosh)

00:14:55.423 --> 00:14:58.368
- Yeah, I mean I was very fortunate.

00:14:58.368 --> 00:15:00.224
I started trying to work at 14,

00:15:00.224 --> 00:15:03.224
and I got on a sitcom when I was 16.

00:15:04.614 --> 00:15:06.046
I dealt with a lot of nos,

00:15:06.046 --> 00:15:08.386
mostly personal nos within my family.

00:15:08.386 --> 00:15:09.785
It's hard for a 14 year old to say,

00:15:09.785 --> 00:15:10.618
"I'm leaving Kentucky.

00:15:10.618 --> 00:15:12.583
"I'm moving to New York."

00:15:12.583 --> 00:15:14.743
But I knew I felt so strongly.

00:15:14.743 --> 00:15:17.982
It feels insane to be a teenager and know.

00:15:17.982 --> 00:15:19.828
I knew that it was the right thing to do.

00:15:19.828 --> 00:15:21.488
I knew that it was going to work out.

00:15:21.488 --> 00:15:22.321
- What did they say?
- Well, "No."

00:15:22.321 --> 00:15:24.550
I wouldn't be supportive
if my teenager was like,

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.633
"I'm moving to New York."

00:15:27.788 --> 00:15:28.920
They thought I was going to get killed.

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.266
I'm surprised I didn't get killed.

00:15:32.266 --> 00:15:36.433
But it was like this fire,
and also I just knew it.

00:15:37.909 --> 00:15:39.583
So I eventually saved up babysitting money

00:15:39.583 --> 00:15:41.641
and went and did it.

00:15:41.641 --> 00:15:43.468
- [Interviewer] So what as the first time

00:15:43.468 --> 00:15:46.362
you felt really challenged
to come up with something

00:15:46.362 --> 00:15:49.449
that was that much harder to do for you?

00:15:49.449 --> 00:15:50.282
- What do you mean?

00:15:50.282 --> 00:15:51.115
- [Interviewer] Well, I mean it seems like

00:15:51.115 --> 00:15:52.705
something like The Burning
Plain for instance,

00:15:52.705 --> 00:15:53.784
it's a really tough role.

00:15:53.784 --> 00:15:55.277
It's not a, you know,

00:15:55.277 --> 00:15:56.110
"I came up.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:15:57.130
"(mumbles) found me in
the street of New York

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:59.148
"and told me to be an
actress and here I am."

00:15:59.148 --> 00:16:00.037
- Yeah.
- Or was it?

00:16:00.037 --> 00:16:02.724
I mean was it just that natural to you?

00:16:02.724 --> 00:16:03.682
- It was.

00:16:03.682 --> 00:16:05.160
I think it actually helped me,

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:07.521
'cause I didn't really go to any classes

00:16:07.521 --> 00:16:10.468
or theater schools or anything like that,

00:16:10.468 --> 00:16:13.198
so I didn't really know how to act.

00:16:13.198 --> 00:16:17.545
I just knew how to talk,
and then I just knew...

00:16:17.545 --> 00:16:18.378
I don't know.

00:16:18.378 --> 00:16:22.136
I think I was able to
build my instincts better,

00:16:22.136 --> 00:16:24.772
and I learned from a lot of mistakes.

00:16:24.772 --> 00:16:26.117
I can look back on things now and be like,

00:16:26.117 --> 00:16:30.092
"Well, at least you learned from that."

00:16:30.092 --> 00:16:31.559
I don't know, I think it was...

00:16:31.559 --> 00:16:32.558
It's so hard for me to answer

00:16:32.558 --> 00:16:34.835
'cause I haven't thought about it

00:16:34.835 --> 00:16:35.948
as much as you guys have. (laughs)

00:16:35.948 --> 00:16:38.083
- [Interviewer] That's probably true.

